{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And you, bring near to yourself.</b> The reason for His saying <b>ואתה</b> (\"and you\"), and likewise His use of the language of הקרבה (bringing near), and likewise His word <b>אליך</b> (\"to yourself\"), will be explained according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 3:17) that the priesthood was originally designated for Moses; only because he refused to go in the matter of HaShem after several instances, HaShem grew angry with him and removed him from the greatness of priesthood, saying (Exodus 4:14): ״הֲלֹא אַהֲרֹן אָחִיךָ הַלֵּוִי״ — meaning that he was a Levite, and now he becomes a priest — end of quote. Therefore He said to him at the time of the action <b>ואתה</b> — meaning, not only because I am commanding you, but also you, from your own side, <b>הקרב</b> (bring near) Aaron in your place, and act as though you desire it yourself, for this is in your stead as an offering to atone for your having hardened your face before HaShem. And His saying <b>אליך</b> is for your atonement: by bringing near Aaron and conferring greatness upon him, there is no offering greater than this for you." "<b>Further</b>, it hints by saying <b>הקרב אליך</b> (\"bring near to yourself\") that through a person's sin, the branch of his soul corresponding to that branch in which he sinned becomes distanced from its root — the great one according to its greatness, and the small one, etc. Now, through the medium of the insistent refusals he made regarding the mission of HaShem, he caused one branch of the branches of his soul to become distanced from its root. And even though HaShem punished him, nevertheless, as long as he had not accepted the punishment, the blemish remains in its place and the distanced one is still distanced. Therefore, at the time of the culmination of the matter, He informed him that through this the distanced one would be brought near — and this is His saying <b>ואתה הקרב אליך</b>, meaning: bring near to yourself, meaning to your own aspect that was distanced through the medium of that sin. And HaShem intended to inform him that this is its rectification (Exodus 4:13)." "And from this I gain understanding regarding a matter that our Sages of blessed memory said at the end of Berakhot (54b): a person is obligated to bless over the bad just as he blesses over the good, etc. And the Gemara explains there (60b): to accept the bad with joy just as one rejoices over the good — end of quote. Now the matter was somewhat distant in my eyes, but according to the things stated regarding this matter, behold — sufferings bring near the branches of the soul that were distanced from their root, for there is no evil and bitter thing like abandoning HaShem one's Elohim, and through the medium of sufferings the distanced one is brought near, and upon this the heart of the understanding one shall rejoice. And He said <b>ועשית בגדי קדש</b> (\"and you shall make holy garments\") etc. <b>לכבוד ולתפארת</b> (\"for honor and for splendor\") — these are themselves signs of joy, that he should show that he desires and speaks to glorify him; this is hateful to one who does a thing only under compulsion. And even though the garments are an obligation incumbent upon the one who stands to minister, with all this HaShem wished to grant him merit to roll this merit through his hand — that he should make them with love and joy for his brother, and this matter is an aspect of soul for the soul, essentially in the doing of this deed. Or perhaps HaShem intended to tell him that he should make the garments from his own property; and even though we learned (Mishnah Yoma 3:7) that the priestly garments come from the public, they did say that an individual may donate them and hand them over to the public — and so HaShem commanded to make them."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:34:08.322555461Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}